# Akamai → Cloudflare Rule Migration — Manual Walkthrough

This README exists so that, if/when this gets built, there is a ground-truth
example of what "correct output" looks like at every stage — derived by hand,
not by code. Use it to sanity-check any implementation later: feed it the
same input tree and diff against this document.

No code below. Every step is done manually against one small example tree.

---

## The example input

A deliberately small Akamai Property Manager rule tree, picked to exercise:
non-terminating behaviors (origin, caching), a terminating behavior (deny),
another terminating behavior (redirect), an `ANY` criteria block, and one
subset relationship (`/api/admin/` ⊂ `/api/`).

```
default (root)
├── criteriaMustSatisfy: all
├── criteria: []
├── behaviors: [origin = OriginDefault, caching = 1h]
│
├── Rule A: "API Rules"
│   ├── criteriaMustSatisfy: all
│   ├── criteria: [path starts_with "/api/"]
│   ├── behaviors: [origin = OriginAPI]
│   │
│   ├── Rule A1: "API Admin Lockdown"
│   │   ├── criteria: [path starts_with "/api/admin/"]
│   │   └── behaviors: [deny]
│   │
│   └── Rule A2: "API Mirror Hosts"
│       ├── criteriaMustSatisfy: any
│       ├── criteria: [hostname = "a.com", hostname = "b.com"]
│       └── behaviors: [caching = 10m]
│
└── Rule B: "Retire Old Blog"
    ├── criteriaMustSatisfy: all
    ├── criteria: [path starts_with "/blog/old/"]
    └── behaviors: [redirect -> "/blog/"]
```

---

## Step 1 — Classify behaviors

Before touching the tree, tag every behavior that appears by hand:

| Behavior | Class |
|---|---|
| `origin` | non-terminating |
| `caching` | non-terminating |
| `deny` | **Akamai-terminating** |
| `redirect` | **Akamai-terminating** |

Nothing here is unrecognized, so nothing gets flagged `UNKNOWN` for this
example. (In a real property, this table is where most of the manual
grinding happens — every behavior name must show up here before you trust
anything downstream.)

---

## Step 2 — DFS: effective criteria, state, order, reachability

Walked by hand, root to leaves. `reachable = false` means: don't emit this
node, because an ancestor already terminates on any request that would
reach it.

| Node | DFS order | Effective criteria | Effective state | Reachable? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| root | 0 | `TRUE` | `origin=OriginDefault, caching=1h` | yes |
| Rule A | 1 | `path starts "/api/"` | `origin=OriginAPI, caching=1h` | yes |
| Rule A1 | 2 | `path starts "/api/"` AND `path starts "/api/admin/"` | `origin=OriginAPI, caching=1h, deny` | yes |
| Rule A2 | 3 | `path starts "/api/"` AND `(hostname="a.com" OR hostname="b.com")` | `origin=OriginAPI, caching=10m` | yes |
| Rule B | 4 | `path starts "/blog/old/"` | `origin=OriginDefault, caching=1h, redirect->/blog/` | yes |

Note: Rule A1 is itself reachable (its parent, Rule A, doesn't terminate),
but Rule A1 *terminates on its own match* via `deny`. It has no children
here, so pruning doesn't visibly matter in this example — but flag it:
**this is the open question from the last round.** If Akamai truly prunes
descendant evaluation under a matched terminator, and Rule A1 *did* have
children, those children would need `reachable = false`. Confirm this
against Akamai's docs before trusting this column on a real property.

---

## Step 3 — Flatten into Semantic Rules

Every reachable node becomes one row. This is just the table above, renamed:

```
Rule#0  order=0  criteria=TRUE                                    state={origin:OriginDefault, caching:1h}
Rule#1  order=1  criteria=path^"/api/"                             state={origin:OriginAPI, caching:1h}
Rule#2  order=2  criteria=path^"/api/" AND path^"/api/admin/"      state={origin:OriginAPI, caching:1h, deny:true}
Rule#3  order=3  criteria=path^"/api/" AND (host=a.com OR host=b.com)  state={origin:OriginAPI, caching:10m}
Rule#4  order=4  criteria=path^"/blog/old/"                        state={origin:OriginDefault, caching:1h, redirect:"/blog/"}
```

(`^` = starts-with, for brevity.)

---

## Step 4 — Normalize criteria

Look for anything collapsible. Here, nothing simplifies — no `AND(X, TRUE)`,
no duplicate terms, no `OR(X, FALSE)`. Rule#2's criteria technically implies
`path^"/api/admin/"` alone is sufficient (since `/api/admin/` already implies
`/api/`), but we do **not** auto-drop the redundant term — that's a job for
the subset analysis in Step 8, not blind AST simplification. Leave it as-is.

---

## Step 5 — Split behaviors from state

```
Rule#0 → [origin: OriginDefault]  [caching: 1h]
Rule#1 → [origin: OriginAPI]
Rule#2 → [origin: OriginAPI]      [caching: 1h]      [deny]
Rule#3 → [origin: OriginAPI]      [caching: 10m]
Rule#4 → [origin: OriginDefault]  [caching: 1h]      [redirect: "/blog/"]
```

Note Rule#1 didn't re-state `caching` since it didn't change it at that
node — but per the effective-state table in Step 2, it still *carries*
`caching:1h` forward for any downstream consumer. Whether you emit that as
a no-op behavior row or leave it implicit is a judgment call; here we only
split behaviors the node **itself declared**, since re-emitting inherited
values that never changed just adds noise to the phase grouping in Step 7.

---

## Step 6 — Map behaviors to Cloudflare phases

| Behavior | Coverage | CF phase | CF action | CF-terminating? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `origin` | mapped | `http_request_origin` | `route` | no |
| `caching` | mapped | `http_request_cache_settings` | `set_cache_settings` | no |
| `deny` | mapped | `http_request_firewall_custom` | `block` | **yes** |
| `redirect` | mapped | `http_request_dynamic_redirect` | `redirect` | **yes** |

(`cf_terminating` here is sourced from Cloudflare's actual documented
behavior: Block/Redirect/challenge actions stop phase evaluation;
everything else is last-match-wins within the phase.)

---

## Step 7 — Group by phase

```
http_request_origin:
    Rule#0 (order 0) → OriginDefault
    Rule#1 (order 1) → OriginAPI
    Rule#2 (order 2) → OriginAPI
    Rule#3 (order 3) → OriginAPI
    Rule#4 (order 4) → OriginDefault

http_request_cache_settings:
    Rule#0 (order 0) → 1h
    Rule#2 (order 2) → 1h
    Rule#3 (order 3) → 10m
    Rule#4 (order 4) → 1h

http_request_firewall_custom:
    Rule#2 (order 2) → block

http_request_dynamic_redirect:
    Rule#4 (order 4) → redirect "/blog/"
```

---

## Step 8 — Overlap analysis

Only pairs whose criteria can actually match the same request matter.
By hand:

- Rule#1 vs Rule#2: `/api/admin/` ⊂ `/api/` → **Rule#2 is more specific.**
  Both use only path-prefix criteria, which is in the decidable vocabulary
  — this one doesn't need a manual-review flag.
- Rule#1 vs Rule#3: Rule#3's criteria (`path^/api/` AND host∈{a,b}) is a
  strict subset of Rule#1's criteria set → **Rule#3 is more specific.**
  Also decidable (path-prefix + hostname-exact only).
- Rule#0 vs everything: root's `TRUE` criteria trivially overlaps all of
  them and is always the least specific.
- Rule#4 vs Rules#0–3: disjoint (`/blog/old/` vs `/api/*`) — no edge needed.

No `OVERLAP_UNKNOWN` cases in this example — small mercy of a clean sample
tree. On a real property, expect this column to have real entries; each one
goes into the migration report as a manual-review item, not a silent
DFS-order fallback.

---

## Step 9–10 — Precedence graph → final order per phase

**`http_request_origin`** (non-terminating → last-match-wins → general
must fire *before* specific, so specific overwrites it):

```
Rule#0 → Rule#1 → Rule#2
Rule#0 → Rule#1 → Rule#3
```
Rule#4 is unrelated (disjoint criteria) — its position relative to the
others doesn't matter, but it must still exist somewhere in the list.

Resolved order: `Rule#0, Rule#1, Rule#2, Rule#3, Rule#4` — DFS order
already happens to satisfy every edge here, so no reordering was actually
needed. That won't always be true; it's a coincidence of this example, not
a shortcut you can assume.

**`http_request_cache_settings`**: same graph shape, same resolution —
`Rule#0, Rule#2, Rule#3, Rule#4`.

**`http_request_firewall_custom`**: only one rule (`Rule#2`) — nothing to
order.

**`http_request_dynamic_redirect`**: only one rule (`Rule#4`) — nothing to
order.

No cycles anywhere in this example → proceed.

---

## Step 11 — Cloudflare expressions (by hand)

```
Rule#0: true
Rule#1: lower(http.request.uri.path) matches "^/api/"
Rule#2: lower(http.request.uri.path) matches "^/api/admin/"
Rule#3: lower(http.request.uri.path) matches "^/api/" and
        (http.host eq "a.com" or http.host eq "b.com")
Rule#4: lower(http.request.uri.path) matches "^/blog/old/"
```

